	<header class=3D"entry-header">
		<h1 class=3D"entry-title">Speeding up modular exponentiation using&nbsp;C=
RT</h1>

				<div class=3D"entry-meta">
						<span class=3D"sep">Posted on </span><a href=3D"https://exploringnumb=
ertheory.wordpress.com/2015/11/16/speeding-up-modular-exponentiation-using-=
crt/" title=3D"10:04 pm" rel=3D"bookmark"><time class=3D"entry-date" dateti=
me=3D"2015-11-16T22:04:09-07:00">November 16, 2015</time></a><span class=3D=
"by-author"> <span class=3D"sep"> by </span> <span class=3D"author vcard"><=
a class=3D"url fn n" href=3D"https://exploringnumbertheory.wordpress.com/au=
thor/exploringnumbertheory/" title=3D"View all posts by Dan Ma" rel=3D"auth=
or">Dan Ma</a></span></span>		</div><!-- .entry-meta -->
			</header><!-- .entry-header -->

	<div class=3D"entry-content">
		<p>This is the fifth post in a series of posts on the Chinese remainder t=
heorem (CRT). When solving a congruence equation with a composite modulus, =
it is often easier to convert the problem to one of solving several congrue=
nce equations with smaller moduli that are primes or powers of primes. Then=
 combine the individual solutions using the Chinese remainder theorem. In t=
his post, we demonstrate this process for modular exponentiation <img src=
=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5Ed+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bm=
od%7D+%5C+m%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" =
alt=3D"x \equiv c^d \ (\text{mod} \ m)" class=3D"latex"> where the exponent=
 <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=
=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d" class=3D"latex"> and the mo=
dulus <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp=
;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m" class=3D"latex"> are lar=
ge. Given <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5Ed+=
%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&=
amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c^d \ (\text{mod} \ m)" class=3D"latex">,=
 the algorithm discussed here is to produce a system of equations with smal=
ler moduli and exponents that give the same answer as for the original prob=
lem.</p>
<p>The previous posts in the series on CRT: <a href=3D"https://exploringnum=
bertheory.wordpress.com/2015/11/15/another-look-at-the-fundamental-theorem-=
of-arithmetic/" target=3D"_blank">first post</a>; <a href=3D"https://explor=
ingnumbertheory.wordpress.com/2015/11/15/versions-of-the-chinese-remainder-=
theorem/" target=3D"_blank">second post</a>; <a href=3D"https://exploringnu=
mbertheory.wordpress.com/2015/11/15/how-to-chinese-remainder-part-1/" targe=
t=3D"_blank">third post</a>; <a href=3D"https://exploringnumbertheory.wordp=
ress.com/2015/11/15/how-to-chinese-remainder-part-2/" target=3D"_blank">fou=
rth post</a></p>
<p>________________________________________________________________________=
____</p>
<p align=3D"center"> <em><strong>Preliminary discussion</strong></em> </p>
<p>The exponentiation <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc%5Ed=
&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"c^d" cl=
ass=3D"latex"> is usually programmed using <a href=3D"https://exploringnumb=
ertheory.wordpress.com/2013/08/04/congruence-arithmetic-and-fast-powering-a=
lgorithm/" target=3D"_blank">the fast powering algorithm</a>. The CRT metho=
d will convert the exponentiation <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?l=
atex=3Dc%5Ed&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" al=
t=3D"c^d" class=3D"latex"> to several exponentiations that involve much sma=
ller exponents and moduli, thus greatly reducing the calculation time, in p=
articular speeding up the fast powering algorithm. One application of the C=
RT method is to improve the run time of the decryption process in the RSA a=
lgorithm (up to four times faster).</p>
<p>As already mentioned, the goal of the algorithm discussed here is to pro=
duce an equivalent system of linear congruence equations. Once this system =
is produced, the Chinese remainder theorem only guarantees a solution and d=
oes not actually produce a solution. So we need to know how to Chinese rema=
inder, i.e. using an algorithm for solving a simultaneous linear congruence=
s. We can use the one discussed <a href=3D"https://exploringnumbertheory.wo=
rdpress.com/2015/11/15/how-to-chinese-remainder-part-1/" target=3D"_blank">=
here (the first method)</a> or <a href=3D"https://exploringnumbertheory.wor=
dpress.com/2015/11/15/how-to-chinese-remainder-part-2/" target=3D"_blank">h=
ere (the second method)</a>. The following examples are worked using the se=
cond method.</p>
<p>The CRT algorithm discussed here makes use of <a href=3D"https://explori=
ngnumbertheory.wordpress.com/2013/08/31/eulers-phi-function-part-1/" target=
=3D"_blank">Euler=E2=80=99s theorem</a>, which states that <img src=3D"http=
s://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Da%5E%7B%5Cphi%28m%29%7D+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%=
5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D=
20201002" alt=3D"a^{\phi(m)} \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod} \ m)" class=3D"latex"> =
whenever <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Da&amp;bg=3Dffffff&=
amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"a" class=3D"latex"> and =
the modulus <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm&amp;bg=3Dffff=
ff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m" class=3D"latex"> a=
re relatively prime where <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%=
5Cphi%28m%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" al=
t=3D"\phi(m)" class=3D"latex"> is the phi function evaluated at <img src=3D=
"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;=
s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m" class=3D"latex">. For this reason, the al=
gorithm requires <a href=3D"https://exploringnumbertheory.wordpress.com/201=
5/11/13/eulers-phi-function-is-multiplicative/" target=3D"_blank">the evalu=
ation of the phi function</a>.</p>
<p>When calculating <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc%5Ed+%=
5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&a=
mp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"c^d \ (\text{mod} \ m)" class=3D"latex">, the use o=
f the phi function <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cphi%2=
8m%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\p=
hi(m)" class=3D"latex"> is to reduce the exponent <img src=3D"https://s0.wp=
.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D=
20201002" alt=3D"d" class=3D"latex"> by the largest multiple of <img src=3D=
"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cphi%28m%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=
=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\phi(m)" class=3D"latex">. For=
 example, since <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cphi%2817=
%29%3D16&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D=
"\phi(17)=3D16" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.ph=
p?latex=3D2%5E%7B16%7D+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+17%29&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"2^{16} \equiv =
1 \ (\text{mod} \ 17)" class=3D"latex">, the problem of <img src=3D"https:/=
/s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D2%5E%7B250%7D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+17%2=
9&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"2^{250=
} \ (\text{mod} \ 17)" class=3D"latex"> is converted to finding  <img src=
=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D2%5E%7B10%7D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7=
D+%5C+17%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"2^{10} \ (\text{mod} \ 17)" class=3D"latex">. Here, the original expone=
nt of 250 is reduced to 10 after taking out the largest multiple of 16. Not=
e that <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D10+%5Cequiv+250+%5C+=
%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+%5Cphi%2817%29%3D16%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333=
333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"10 \equiv 250 \ (\text{mod} \ \phi(1=
7)=3D16)" class=3D"latex">. In general, we want to replace the original exp=
onent <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp=
;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d" class=3D"latex"> by a sm=
aller exponent <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd_1&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d_1" class=3D"=
latex"> where <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd_1+%5Cequiv+=
d+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+%5Cphi%28m%29%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333=
333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d_1 \equiv d \ (\text{mod} \ \phi(m)=
)" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<div class=3D"wordads-ad-wrapper"><div class=3D"wordads-ad"><div class=3D"w=
ordads-ad-title">Advertisement</div><div class=3D"wordads-ad-content wordad=
s-ad-inline" id=3D"wordads-ad-664549"></div><div class=3D"wordads-ad-contro=
ls"></div></div></div><p>__________________________________________________=
__________________________</p>
<p align=3D"center"> <em><strong>Examples</strong></em> </p>
<p>In the following three examples, we use the algorithm <a href=3D"https:/=
/exploringnumbertheory.wordpress.com/2015/11/15/how-to-chinese-remainder-pa=
rt-2/" target=3D"_blank">discussed here</a> to solve systems of linear cong=
ruence equations (this is the iterative approach). These examples are by no=
 means realistic since the numbers used are small. So they are for demonstr=
ation of how CRT works.</p>
<p><strong><em>Example 1</em></strong><br>
Calculate <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+2%5E%7=
B3163%7D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+3969%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D33333=
3&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 2^{3163} \ (\text{mod} \ 3969=
)" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<p>First, factor the modulus <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=
=3D3969%3D3%5E4+%5Ctimes+7%5E2%3D81+%5Ctimes+49&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D33=
3333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"3969=3D3^4 \times 7^2=3D81 \times 4=
9" class=3D"latex">. Now the problem is converted to solving the following =
system of two equations: </p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+2%5E%7B3163%7D+%=
5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+81%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&=
amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 2^{3163} \ (\text{mod} \ 81)" class=3D"la=
tex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+2%5E%7B3163%7=
D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+49%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 2^{3163} \ (\text{mod} \ 49)" class=
=3D"latex">
</p></ul>
<p>By CRT, any solution to the two equations is also a solution to the orig=
inal equation. However, the exponent of 3163 should first be reduced. To th=
is end, calculate the phi function, where <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/lat=
ex.php?latex=3D%5Cphi%283%5E4%29%3D3%5E2+%5Ccdot+%283-1%29%3D54&amp;bg=3Dff=
ffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\phi(3^4)=3D3^2 \cd=
ot (3-1)=3D54" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php=
?latex=3D%5Cphi%287%5E2%29%3D7+%5Ccdot+%287-1%29%3D42&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;f=
g=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\phi(7^2)=3D7 \cdot (7-1)=3D4=
2" class=3D"latex">. We should reduce from 3163 the largest multiple of 54 =
in the first equation and reduce the largest multiple of 42 in the second e=
quation. In other words, reduce the exponent 3163 modulo the two phi functi=
on values:</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D3163+%5Cequiv+31+%5C+%28%5C=
text%7Bmod%7D+%5C+54%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"3163 \equiv 31 \ (\text{mod} \ 54)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D3163+%5Cequiv+13+%5C+%28=
%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+42%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=
=3D20201002" alt=3D"3163 \equiv 13 \ (\text{mod} \ 42)" class=3D"latex"></p=
>
</ul>
<p>As a result, we solve the following two equations:</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+2%5E%7B3163%7D+%=
5Cequiv+2%5E%7B31%7D+%5Cequiv+65+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+81%29&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 2^{31=
63} \equiv 2^{31} \equiv 65 \ (\text{mod} \ 81)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+2%5E%7B3163%7=
D+%5Cequiv+2%5E%7B13%7D+%5Cequiv+9+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+49%29&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 2^{31=
63} \equiv 2^{13} \equiv 9 \ (\text{mod} \ 49)" class=3D"latex">
</p></ul>
<p>Note that the original exponentiation <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/late=
x.php?latex=3D2%5E%7B3163%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;=
c=3D20201002" alt=3D"2^{3163}" class=3D"latex"> is turned into the easier o=
nes of <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D2%5E%7B31%7D&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"2^{31}" class=
=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D2%5E%7B13%7=
D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"2^{13}=
" class=3D"latex">. The following gives the solution to the above two equat=
ions.</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cdisplaystyle+%5Cbegin%7B=
aligned%7D+x_0%26%3D65%2B81+%5Ccdot+23+%5Ccdot+%289-65%29+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7B=
mod%7D+%5C+3969%29++%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+-104263+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+3=
969%29+%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+2900+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+3969%29+%5Cend%7B=
aligned%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"\displaystyle \begin{aligned} x_0&amp;=3D65+81 \cdot 23 \cdot (9-65) \ =
(\text{mod} \ 3969)  \\&amp;\equiv -104263 \ (\text{mod} \ 3969) \\&amp;\eq=
uiv 2900 \ (\text{mod} \ 3969) \end{aligned}" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p>where 23 is obtained by solving for <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.=
php?latex=3Dy&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" a=
lt=3D"y" class=3D"latex"> in <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=
=3D81y+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+49%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=
=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"81y \equiv 1 \ (\text{mod} \ 4=
9)" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>By CRT, the answer to the original problem is <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.=
com/latex.php?latex=3D2%5E%7B3163%7D+%5Cequiv+2900+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+=
%5C+3969%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"2^{3163} \equiv 2900 \ (\text{mod} \ 3969)" class=3D"latex">. <img src=
=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Csquare&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D=
333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\square" class=3D"latex"></p>
<div class=3D"wordads-ad-wrapper" style=3D"display: inherit;"><div id=3D"at=
atags-26942-565623"></div></div><p><strong><em>Example 2</em></strong><br>
Calculate <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+3%5E%7=
B3163%7D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+3969%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D33333=
3&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 3^{3163} \ (\text{mod} \ 3969=
)" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<p>In this example, the number 3 and the modulus 3969 are not relatively pr=
ime. The CRT method still applies. As in Example 1, the problem can be redu=
ced in the following way:</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+3%5E%7B3163%7D+%=
5Cequiv+3%5E%7B31%7D+%5Cequiv+0+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+81%29&amp;bg=3D=
ffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 3^{3163}=
 \equiv 3^{31} \equiv 0 \ (\text{mod} \ 81)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+2%5E%7B3163%7=
D+%5Cequiv+3%5E%7B13%7D+%5Cequiv+10+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+49%29&amp;b=
g=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 2^{3=
163} \equiv 3^{13} \equiv 10 \ (\text{mod} \ 49)" class=3D"latex">
</p></ul>
<p>The first equation is congruent to 0 since <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com=
/latex.php?latex=3D3%5E%7B31%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&a=
mp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"3^{31}" class=3D"latex"> contains 81 as a factor. T=
he following gives the solution to the above two equations:</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cdisplaystyle+%5Cbegin%7B=
aligned%7D+x_0%26%3D0%2B81+%5Ccdot+23+%5Ccdot+%2810-0%29+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bm=
od%7D+%5C+3969%29++%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+18630+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+3969=
%29+%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+2754+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+3969%29+%5Cend%7Bali=
gned%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"=
\displaystyle \begin{aligned} x_0&amp;=3D0+81 \cdot 23 \cdot (10-0) \ (\tex=
t{mod} \ 3969)  \\&amp;\equiv 18630 \ (\text{mod} \ 3969) \\&amp;\equiv 275=
4 \ (\text{mod} \ 3969) \end{aligned}" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
</ul>
<p>By CRT, the answer to the original problem is <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.=
com/latex.php?latex=3D3%5E%7B3163%7D+%5Cequiv+2754+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+=
%5C+3969%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"3^{3163} \equiv 2754 \ (\text{mod} \ 3969)" class=3D"latex">. <img src=
=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Csquare&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D=
333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\square" class=3D"latex"></p>
<p><strong><em>Example 3</em></strong><br>
The above 2 examples use small numbers to illustrate the CRT technique. In =
this example, we use slightly larger numbers. Calculate <img src=3D"https:/=
/s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+355%5E%7Bd%7D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%=
7D+%5C+m%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"x \equiv 355^{d} \ (\text{mod} \ m)" class=3D"latex"> where <img src=3D=
"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd%3D%5Ctext%7B1%2C759%2C695%2C794%7D&a=
mp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d=3D\text=
{1,759,695,794}" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.p=
hp?latex=3Dm%3D%5Ctext%7B3%2C055%2C933%2C789%7D%3D1277+%5Ccdot+1439+%5Ccdot=
+1663&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m=
=3D\text{3,055,933,789}=3D1277 \cdot 1439 \cdot 1663" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<p>As in the other examples, we break up the problem in three congruences. =
The three factors of the modulus are prime numbers. Thus we reduce the expo=
nent <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;=
fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d" class=3D"latex"> by multi=
ples of a prime factor less one.</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Ctext%7B1%2C759%2C695%2C7=
94%7D+%5Cequiv+1198+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+1276%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp=
;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\text{1,759,695,794} \equiv=
 1198 \ (\text{mod} \ 1276)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Ctext%7B1%2C759%2C695%=
2C794%7D+%5Cequiv+814+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+1438%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&a=
mp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\text{1,759,695,794} \equ=
