<p align=3D"center"> <em><strong>The CRT algorithm to speed exponentiation<=
/strong></em> </p>
<p>Suppose we wish to evaluate <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?late=
x=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5E%7Bd%7D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff=
&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c^{d} \ (\text=
{mod} \ m)" class=3D"latex"> where the prime factorization of <img src=3D"h=
ttps://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m" class=3D"latex"> is <img src=3D"https://s0=
.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm%3Dp_1%5E%7Bn_1%7D+%5Ccdot+p_2%5E%7Bn_2%7D+%5Ccd=
ots+p_t%5E%7Bn_t%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201=
002" alt=3D"m=3Dp_1^{n_1} \cdot p_2^{n_2} \cdots p_t^{n_t}" class=3D"latex"=
>. The numbers <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dp_i&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"p_i" class=3D"=
latex"> are distinct prime numbers and each exponent <img src=3D"https://s0=
.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dn_i+%5Cge+1&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"n_i \ge 1" class=3D"latex">. To prepare for t=
he calculation, do the following:</p>
<ul>
Let <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm_i%3Dp_i%5E%7Bn_i%7D&a=
mp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m_i=3Dp_i=
^{n_i}" class=3D"latex"> for each <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?l=
atex=3Di&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D=
"i" class=3D"latex">. <p></p>
<p>Calculate <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cphi%28m_i%2=
9&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\phi(m=
_i)" class=3D"latex"> for each <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?late=
x=3Di&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"i"=
 class=3D"latex">.</p>
</ul>
<p><em><strong>Case 1</strong></em>. The base <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com=
/latex.php?latex=3Dc&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2020=
1002" alt=3D"c" class=3D"latex"> and the modulus <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.=
com/latex.php?latex=3Dm&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"m" class=3D"latex"> are relatively prime.<br>
Then the answer to the original exponentiation problem is identical to the =
solution to the following system of <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php=
?latex=3Dt&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"t" class=3D"latex"> congruence equations:</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5E%7Bd_1%7D+%5=
C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_1%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&=
amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c^{d_1} \ (\text{mod} \ m_1)" class=3D"la=
tex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5E%7Bd_2%7D=
+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_2%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c^{d_2} \ (\text{mod} \ m_2)" class=
=3D"latex"></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Ccdots&amp;bg=3Dffffff=
&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\cdots" class=3D"latex"=
></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5E%7Bd_t%7D=
+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_t%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c^{d_t} \ (\text{mod} \ m_t)" class=
=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>where <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd_i+%5Cequiv+d+%5C=
+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+%5Cphi%28m_i%29%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333=
&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d_i \equiv d \ (\text{mod} \ \phi(m_i))=
" class=3D"latex"> for each <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=
=3Di&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"i" =
class=3D"latex">. If possible, each <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php=
?latex=3Dc%5E%7Bd_i%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20=
201002" alt=3D"c^{d_i}" class=3D"latex"> should be reduced modulo <img src=
=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm_i&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333=
&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m_i" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<p><em><strong>Case 2</strong></em>. The base <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com=
/latex.php?latex=3Dc&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2020=
1002" alt=3D"c" class=3D"latex"> and the modulus <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.=
com/latex.php?latex=3Dm&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"m" class=3D"latex"> are not relatively prime.<br>
In this case, <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc&amp;bg=3Dff=
ffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"c" class=3D"latex">=
 and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;=
fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m" class=3D"latex"> have pri=
me factors in common (at least one <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?=
latex=3Dp_i&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"p_i" class=3D"latex">). The idea here is that for any <img src=3D"https=
://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dp_i&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D=
0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"p_i" class=3D"latex"> that is a prime factor of =
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D=
333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"c" class=3D"latex">, the equation=
 <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5E%7Bd_i%7D+%=
5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_i%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0=
&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c^{d_i} \ (\text{mod} \ m_i)" class=3D"l=
atex"> in Case 1 is replaced by <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?lat=
ex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+0+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_i%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=
=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 0 \ (\text{mod} \ m_i=
)" class=3D"latex">. Then solve the resulting system of equations (see Exam=
ple 2). Essentially, Case 2 can fall under Case 1 with <img src=3D"https://=
s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc%5E%7Bd_i%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&a=
mp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"c^{d_i}" class=3D"latex"> being congruent=
 to zero. We call out Case 2 for the sake of clarity.</p>
<p>The original exponentiation <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?late=
x=3Dc%5Ed&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"c^d" class=3D"latex"> boils down to solving an appropriate system of CR=
T congruences as described above. Once the equivalent system of congruences=
 is set up, use the algorithm discussed <a href=3D"https://exploringnumbert=
heory.wordpress.com/2015/11/15/how-to-chinese-remainder-part-1/" target=3D"=
_blank">here</a> or <a href=3D"https://exploringnumbertheory.wordpress.com/=
2015/11/15/how-to-chinese-remainder-part-2/" target=3D"_blank">here</a> to =
do Chinese remaindering.</p>
<p><em><strong>Comment</strong></em><br>
Both Case 1 and Case 2 produce a system of linear congruence equations that=
 have identical solution to the original equation. This is a result of usin=
g CRT (see <a href=3D"https://exploringnumbertheory.wordpress.com/2015/11/1=
5/versions-of-the-chinese-remainder-theorem/" target=3D"_blank">Theorem D a=
nd Theorem G here</a>). The savings in the calculation come in the form of =
the smaller exponentiations in the resulting congruence equations.</p>
<div class=3D"wordads-ad-wrapper" style=3D"display: inherit;"><div id=3D"at=
atags-26942-433274"></div></div><p>In Case 2, some of the congruence equati=
ons are <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+0&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv 0" cl=
ass=3D"latex">. This is because the base <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/late=
x.php?latex=3Dc&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002"=
 alt=3D"c" class=3D"latex"> and some moduli <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/l=
atex.php?latex=3Dm_i&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2020=
1002" alt=3D"m_i" class=3D"latex"> are not relatively prime. For these modu=
li, <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc%5E%7Bd_i%7D&amp;bg=3D=
ffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"c^{d_i}" class=3D=
"latex"> would contain <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dp_i%=
5E%7Bn_i%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"p_i^{n_i}" class=3D"latex"> as a factor (assuming that <img src=3D"http=
s://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd_i+%5Cge+n_i&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D3333=
33&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d_i \ge n_i" class=3D"latex">). Hence=
, <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5E%7Bd_i%7D+=
%5Cequiv+0+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_i%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D3333=
33&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c^{d_i} \equiv 0 \ (\text{mo=
d} \ m_i)" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<p><em><strong>The two-equation case</strong></em><br>
When the modulus is the product of two factors that are relatively prime, t=
he CRT algorithm involves only two equations. We write out the solution exp=
licitly for this case. To evaluate <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?=
latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5E%7Bd%7D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m%29&amp;bg=3Dff=
ffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c^{d} \ (\=
text{mod} \ m)" class=3D"latex"> where <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.=
php?latex=3Dm%3Dm_1+%5Ccdot+m_2&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&a=
mp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m=3Dm_1 \cdot m_2" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D=
"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm_1&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&am=
p;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m_1" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"http=
s://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm_2&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m_2" class=3D"latex"> are relatively prime, s=
olve the following system of two equations,</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5E%7Bd_1%7D+%5=
C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_1%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&=
amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c^{d_1} \ (\text{mod} \ m_1)" class=3D"la=
tex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequiv+c%5E%7Bd_2%7D=
+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m_2%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv c^{d_2} \ (\text{mod} \ m_2)" class=
=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>The solution is <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dx+%5Cequ=
iv++c%5E%7Bd_1%7D%2Bm_1+%5Ccdot+v_1+%5Ccdot+%28c%5E%7Bd_2%7D-c%5E%7Bd_1%7D%=
29+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+m%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"x \equiv  c^{d_1}+m_1 \cdot v_1 \cdot (c^{d_2=
}-c^{d_1}) \ (\text{mod} \ m)" class=3D"latex"> where <img src=3D"https://s=
0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dv_1&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&am=
p;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"v_1" class=3D"latex"> is the multiplicative inverse =
of <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm_1&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;=
fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m_1" class=3D"latex"> modulo=
 <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm_2&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=
=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m_2" class=3D"latex">. If poss=
ible, each <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc%5E%7Bd_i%7D&am=
p;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"c^{d_i}" c=
lass=3D"latex"> should be reduced modulo <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/late=
x.php?latex=3Dm_i&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2020100=
2" alt=3D"m_i" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<p>________________________________________________________________________=
____</p>
<p align=3D"center"> <em><strong>RSA application</strong></em> </p>
<p>The algorithm to speed the exponentiation is possible because the factor=
ization of the modulus <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm&am=
p;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m" class=
=3D"latex"> is known (as a result, the values of <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.=
com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cphi%28m_i%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\phi(m_i)" class=3D"latex"> are known). Knowi=
ng the values of <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cphi%28m=
_i%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\p=
hi(m_i)" class=3D"latex"> makes it possible to reduce the large exponent <i=
mg src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D33=
3333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d" class=3D"latex">. For this reaso=
n, the decryption process in the RSA algorithm is a perfect place to apply =
the CRT technique described here. </p>
<p>With the RSA cryptosystem, a public key consists of <img src=3D"https://=
s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DN&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp=
;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"N" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com=
/latex.php?latex=3De&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2020=
1002" alt=3D"e" class=3D"latex">, where <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex=
.php?latex=3DN&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" =
alt=3D"N" class=3D"latex"> is a large modulus that is a product of two larg=
e primes <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dp&amp;bg=3Dffffff&=
amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"p" class=3D"latex"> and =
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dq&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D=
333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"q" class=3D"latex"> (the two prim=
es are not published) and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3De=
&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"e" clas=
s=3D"latex"> is the encryption exponent. Say Bob is the originator of the R=
SA public key. Bob also generates a private key, which is a number <img src=
=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&a=
mp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d" class=3D"latex"> that is used for decr=
ypting any messages that he receives. The number <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.=
com/latex.php?latex=3Dd&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D2=
0201002" alt=3D"d" class=3D"latex"> must be kept private. The prime factors=
 <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dp&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=
=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"p" class=3D"latex"> and <img s=
rc=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dq&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333=
&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"q" class=3D"latex"> must also be kept s=
ecret since knowing <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dp&amp;b=
g=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"p" class=3D"l=
atex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dq&amp;bg=3Dfffff=
f&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"q" class=3D"latex"> ca=
n derive <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd&amp;bg=3Dffffff&=
amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<p>Suppose that Alice has a message to send to Bob. She can do so using the=
 published key of <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DN&amp;bg=
=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"N" class=3D"la=
tex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3De&amp;bg=3Dffffff=
&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"e" class=3D"latex"> thr=
ough the exponentiation <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc+%=
5Cequiv+m%5Ee+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+N%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333=
333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"c \equiv m^e \ (\text{mod} \ N)" cla=
ss=3D"latex">. Here, <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm&amp;=
bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m" class=3D"=
latex"> is the plaintext (the message to be sent) and <img src=3D"https://s=
0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;=
c=3D20201002" alt=3D"c" class=3D"latex"> is the ciphertext (the encrypted m=
essage). Upon receiving the ciphertext <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.=
php?latex=3Dc&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" a=
lt=3D"c" class=3D"latex">, Bob can then decrypt through the exponentiation =
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm+%5Cequiv+c%5Ed+%5C+%28%5C=
text%7Bmod%7D+%5C+N%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20=
201002" alt=3D"m \equiv c^d \ (\text{mod} \ N)" class=3D"latex"> where <img=
 src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D3333=
33&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d" class=3D"latex"> is the decryption=
 exponent. In realistic RSA calculation, the public modulus <img src=3D"htt=
ps://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DN&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D=
0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"N" class=3D"latex"> and the private decryption e=
xponent <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd&amp;bg=3Dffffff&a=
mp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d" class=3D"latex"> are l=
arge integers (<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3DN&amp;bg=3Df=
fffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"N" class=3D"latex"=
> is at minimum a 2048-bit number). With CRT, the decryption can be reduced=
 to two much smaller exponentiations. The effect can be at least four times=
 faster.</p>
<div class=3D"wordads-ad-wrapper" style=3D"display: inherit;"><div id=3D"at=
atags-26942-123967"></div></div><p>We illustrate this with an example. This=
 is a toy example since the numbers used are small. It is only intended as =
an illustration.</p>
<p><strong><em>Example 4</em></strong><br>
Suppose the public key consists of <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?=
latex=3DN%3D%5Ctext%7B17%2C086%2C049%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp=
;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"N=3D\text{17,086,049}" class=3D"latex"> and=
 <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3De%3D65537&amp;bg=3Dffffff&=
amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"e=3D65537" class=3D"late=
x">. Bob has the additional information of <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/la=
tex.php?latex=3DN%3Dp+%5Ccdot+q&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&a=
mp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"N=3Dp \cdot q" class=3D"latex"> where <img src=3D"h=
ttps://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dp%3D3863&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333=
&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"p=3D3863" class=3D"latex"> and <img src=
=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dq%3D4423&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D3=
33333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"q=3D4423" class=3D"latex">, which =
are kept secret. Knowing <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dp&=
amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"p" class=
=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dq&amp;bg=3D=
ffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"q" class=3D"latex=
"> allows Bob to compute <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dd%=
3D%5Ctext%7B5%2C731%2C241%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;=
c=3D20201002" alt=3D"d=3D\text{5,731,241}" class=3D"latex">. Suppose that B=
ob receives a message <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dc%3D&=
amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"c=3D" cl=
ass=3D"latex"> 4831984 from Alice. Use the CRT approach to find the plainte=
xt <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=
=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m" class=3D"latex">.</p>
<p>The exponentiation is <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm+=
%5Cequiv+4831984%5E%7B5731241%7D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+N%29&amp;bg=3D=
ffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m \equiv 4831984^=
{5731241} \ (\text{mod} \ N)" class=3D"latex">, which is equivalent to the =
following two equations by CRT:</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm+%5Cequiv+4831984%5E%7B573=
1241%7D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+3863%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333=
&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m \equiv 4831984^{5731241} \ (\text{mod=
} \ 3863)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm+%5Cequiv+4831984%5E%7B=
5731241%7D+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+4423%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333=
333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"m \equiv 4831984^{5731241} \ (\text{=
mod} \ 4423)" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>which is further simplified to:</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm+%5Cequiv+4831984%5E%7B573=
1241%7D+%5Cequiv+4831984%5E%7B33%7D+%5Cequiv+3084+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%=
5C+3863%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=
=3D"m \equiv 4831984^{5731241} \equiv 4831984^{33} \equiv 3084 \ (\text{mod=
} \ 3863)" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p><img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3Dm+%5Cequiv+4831984%5E%7B=
5731241%7D+%5Cequiv+4831984%5E%7B329%7D+%5Cequiv+1436+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%=
7D+%5C+4423%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" =
alt=3D"m \equiv 4831984^{5731241} \equiv 4831984^{329} \equiv 1436 \ (\text=
{mod} \ 4423)" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>where <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D33+%5Cequiv+573124=
1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+3862%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=
=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"33 \equiv 5731241 \ (\text{mod} \ 3862)" clas=
s=3D"latex"> and <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D329+%5Cequ=
iv+5731241+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+4422%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333=
333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"329 \equiv 5731241 \ (\text{mod} \ 4=
422)" class=3D"latex">. Then the following is the plaintext (the original m=
essage).</p>
<ul>
<img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Cdisplaystyle+%5Cbegin%7B=
aligned%7D+m%26%3D3084%2B3863+%5Ccdot+1319+%5Ccdot+%281436-3084%29+%5C+%28%=
5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+3863+%5Ccdot+4423%29++%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+3084%2B5095297+=
%5Ccdot+%28-1648%29+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+N%29+%5C%5C%26%5Cequiv+9289=
736+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+N%29+%5Cend%7Baligned%7D&amp;bg=3Dffffff&am=
p;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\displaystyle \begin{align=
ed} m&amp;=3D3084+3863 \cdot 1319 \cdot (1436-3084) \ (\text{mod} \ 3863 \c=
dot 4423)  \\&amp;\equiv 3084+5095297 \cdot (-1648) \ (\text{mod} \ N) \\&a=
mp;\equiv 9289736 \ (\text{mod} \ N) \end{aligned}" class=3D"latex"><p></p>
<p>where 1319 is obtained by solving for <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/late=
x.php?latex=3Dy&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002"=
 alt=3D"y" class=3D"latex"> in <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?late=
x=3D3683y+%5Cequiv+1+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+4423%29&amp;bg=3Dffffff&am=
p;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"3683y \equiv 1 \ (\text{mo=
d} \ 4423)" class=3D"latex"></p>
</ul>
<p>The answer is <img src=3D"https://s0.wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D4831984%5E=
%7B5731241%7D+%5Cequiv+9289736+%5C+%28%5Ctext%7Bmod%7D+%5C+N%29&amp;bg=3Dff=
ffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"4831984^{5731241} \=
equiv 9289736 \ (\text{mod} \ N)" class=3D"latex">. <img src=3D"https://s0.=
wp.com/latex.php?latex=3D%5Csquare&amp;bg=3Dffffff&amp;fg=3D333333&amp;s=3D=
0&amp;c=3D20201002" alt=3D"\square" class=3D"latex"></p>
